@meta
id: 28-07
title: The settings that stop you losing work
chapter: 28
type: lesson
skills: vimrc-structure
par: 268
minutes: 6
vscode: hot exit and local history, both on by default and invisible
open: .vimrc

@brief
Out of the box vim leaves litter beside your files — `order.ts.swp` while
you are editing, `order.ts~` after you save — and forgets every undo the
moment you close the buffer. All three are configurable, and the
interesting one is the third.

Add a section at the end of the config:

    " --- Don't lose work ---
    set undofile              " undo history survives closing the file
    set undodir=~/.vim/undo
    set nobackup              " no file~ next to the original
    set noswapfile            " no .file.swp either

`undofile` is the one you will be grateful for. Reopen a file tomorrow,
press `u`, and yesterday's edits come back — including changes you made
before the last save. `undodir` keeps those history files in one place
instead of scattering them through your repositories; the directory has
to exist, so make it once with `mkdir -p ~/.vim/undo`.

`nobackup` and `noswapfile` are a trade. The swap file is what offers you
recovery after a crash or a lost ssh connection. Most people turn it off
because their work is in git and a stale swap prompt is more annoying
than the crash it protects against. The comment beside each line is where
you record which way you decided.

@keys
| `G` | last line |
| `o` | open a line below |
| `O` | open a line above |
| `:!mkdir -p ~/.vim/undo` | make the directory from inside vim |

@hints
The section goes at the bottom, after everything already there. `G` then
`o` starts you off, and Enter inside insert mode keeps going.
--
Type the four settings first and put the header comment on last. Press
Enter at the end of a comment line and vim starts the next line with a
`"` as well, which is not what you want when the next line is a setting.
--
`G` `o` Enter for the blank line, then the four settings separated by
Enter, then Esc. Then `3k` back to `set undofile` and `O` to open the
header line above it.

@solution
    G o <Enter>
    set undofile              " undo history survives closing the file<Enter>
    set undodir=~/.vim/undo<Enter>
    set nobackup              " no file~ next to the original<Enter>
    set noswapfile            " no .file.swp either <Esc>
    3k O " --- Don't lose work --- <Esc> :wq<Enter>

The header goes on last on purpose. `filetype plugin on` loads vim's own
ftplugin for vim files, and it asks for comment leaders to be continued:
press Enter at the end of a `"` line and the next line starts with `"`
too. Convenient when you are writing a paragraph of comment, wrong when
the next line is a setting. Opening the header separately with `O`, from
a line that is not a comment, sidesteps it entirely. Your own config is
the first place you will meet a filetype plugin doing something on your
behalf. It will not be the last.

Three notes for when you use this for real.

`undodir` takes a list, and a trailing `//` makes vim build the undo
file's name from the file's full path: `set undodir=~/.vim/undo//`. Worth
having the day two files called `index.ts` in different directories share
one history file and you cannot work out why `u` did something strange.

`set undofile` is off by default for a reason — the history files are
written wherever you point them, for every file you edit, including files
in `/tmp` and in other people's repositories. That is fine on your
laptop. On a shared box, think about it.

This section is also the one that breaks first on a server you ssh into,
because `~/.vim/undo` is not there and vim complains on every write. A
config meant to travel guards the lines that assume something exists:

    if has('persistent_undo')
      set undofile
      set undodir=~/.vim/undo
    endif

That is the whole portability trick. One file, no plugins, `has()` around
anything that might be missing, and you can `scp ~/.vimrc` to a box you
will use once and have your own editor on it. It is the strongest
argument for a config you can read in one screen: the 500-line one does
not survive the trip.

And if you keep `swapfile` on, learn what the recovery prompt is telling
you rather than pressing `d` reflexively. `:recover` on a file after a
crash has saved more work than `undofile` ever will.

@start .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set nocompatible
syntax on
filetype plugin indent on

" --- Indentation ---
set autoindent
set expandtab
set shiftwidth=2

@expected .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set nocompatible
syntax on
filetype plugin indent on

" --- Indentation ---
set autoindent
set expandtab
set shiftwidth=2

" --- Don't lose work ---
set undofile              " undo history survives closing the file
set undodir=~/.vim/undo
set nobackup              " no file~ next to the original
set noswapfile            " no .file.swp either
